// rxjs how-to: apply CATCH AND RETHROW pattern to build a custom error from the service BEFORE it gets to the Observer
* by using the catchError AND throwError rxjs from the service AND THEN the EMPTY Observable from the component
* note: @see the previous post for component and template, as they don't change

** service definition example!

return http.get(...)
  .pipe(
    map(...)
	.catchError( err => {
	  handleError(err);
	})
  );
  
handleError(err: any) {
  let errorMessage: string;
  if (err.error instanceof ErrorEvent) {
    errorMessage = `Error occured: ${err.error.message}`;
  } else {
    errorMessage = `From backend: ${err.status} : ${err.body.error}`;
  }
  console.error(err);
  return throwError(errorMessage);	// THAT IS THE IMPORTANT PART!
}


// rxjs performance improvement with ChangeDetectionStrategy.onPush
* Target "pure reactive" components that are updated via Observable streams
* BE CAREFUL WITH THAT because every change to a component property which is not a stream will NOT be updated in the component by using this (OnPush) Change Detection Strategy!!!

@Component({
 ...
 changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.onPush
)}


// rxjs INTERESTING FACT: how is it different than java.util.Stream ?

1) every execution of a *chain of operators* processes a single "apple", before going on with the next "apple" in the stream.
2) the take(n) operator IMO should be placed at the START of the operator chain to avoid useless processing... in other words, the "nth+1 apple" ain't gonna make it til the end anyways.
3) if an error is thrown within an operator, the next "apple" ain't gonna be processed, and the complete callback of the Observer(s) is NOT gonna be called.


// async pipe - alternative to simplify display - just chain the Observable.catch in the UI component.
* It's nice because async handles the subscribe/unsubscribe to the Observable by itself.  Therefore, no need for the component to implement OnDestroy and have sub:Subscription.unsubscribe().

